## CATEGORY 1: USER STORIES & ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
*70 Prompts*

### Writing User Stories

1. Write 5 user stories for a government employee self-service portal where workers can submit time-off requests, view pay stubs, update personal information, and download tax documents. Use the format: As a [role], I want [goal], so that [benefit].

2. I'm building a [system name] for a [agency/organization type]. Write 8 user stories covering the core workflows for a [user role] who needs to [main objective]. Use standard user story format.

3. Convert these business requirements into user stories: [paste requirements]. Maintain the original intent but rewrite each requirement from the end user's perspective using the As a / I want / So that format.

4. Write user stories for a mobile application that allows field inspectors at a state agency to complete inspection checklists, attach photos, add GPS coordinates, and submit reports offline when connectivity is unavailable.

5. I have a large epic: "As a procurement officer, I want a digital contract management system." Decompose this into 8–10 sprint-ready user stories covering: creation, routing, approval, signature, storage, and search.

6. Generate user stories for the Admin role in a case management platform used by a county social services department. Cover: user management, role assignment, audit logging, data exports, and system configuration.

7. Write user stories for a reporting module that allows [user role] to generate standard reports, create custom reports using a drag-and-drop builder, schedule automated delivery, and export to PDF or Excel.

8. I need user stories for a data migration project moving from [old system] to [new system]. Write 6 stories covering: data validation, mapping rules, migration scheduling, reconciliation reporting, error handling, and rollback procedures.

9. Review these user stories and identify which violate the INVEST criteria (Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable). For each violation, explain the issue and rewrite the story: [paste user stories].

10. Write 5 user stories for Section 508 / WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility compliance. Cover: screen reader support, keyboard navigation, color contrast, text resizing, and form field labels.

11. Generate user stories for a citizen-facing online permit application portal. The citizen should be able to: create an account, start an application, save drafts, upload supporting documents, pay fees online, and track application status.

12. Write user stories for the notification system in a case management application. Users should receive email, SMS, and in-app alerts for: status changes, assigned tasks, approaching deadlines, and system messages.

13. I have these stakeholder interview notes about what users need from a new scheduling system: [paste notes]. Extract and write user stories from these notes, grouping related functionality.

14. Write user stories for an audit trail feature that must log all data changes, including: who made the change, what was changed, the old value, the new value, and the timestamp.

15. Create user stories for a document management feature allowing users to: upload, version-control, tag, search, share, and archive documents within a case file.

### Government & Compliance User Stories

16. Write user stories for a FISMA-compliant authentication system. Cover: multi-factor authentication, PIV card support, session timeout, failed login lockout, and password policy enforcement.

17. Generate user stories for a federal grant management system used by state agencies. Cover: grant discovery, application submission, award acceptance, reporting milestones, drawdown requests, and closeout procedures.

18. Write 6 user stories for a Freedom of Information Act (FOIA) request management module. Cover: request intake, routing, tracking, redaction workflow, response generation, and appeals.

19. Create user stories for a records retention module that must: automatically classify records by type, apply retention schedules, flag records for review, and initiate disposition workflows.

20. Write user stories for a state agency procurement portal covering: vendor registration, solicitation publication, bid submission, evaluation scoring, award notification, and contract execution.

21. Generate user stories for a public-facing data portal where citizens can search, view, filter, and download open government datasets. Include accessibility and mobile responsiveness requirements.

22. Write user stories for an employee onboarding workflow at a government agency. Cover: HR setup, IT provisioning, badge issuance, training assignment, mentor assignment, and 90-day check-ins.

23. Create user stories for a compliance tracking module that: monitors regulatory requirements, assigns owners, tracks completion status, and generates compliance dashboards for leadership.

24. Write user stories for a government contract modification workflow covering: request, justification, legal review, CO approval, contractor acknowledgment, and system update.

25. Generate user stories for a background investigation request system used by HR to: initiate, track, and receive results for security clearance investigations.

### Acceptance Criteria — Gherkin Format

26. Write Gherkin acceptance criteria (Given/When/Then) for this user story: As a registered user, I want to reset my password via email so that I can regain access when I forget my credentials.

27. Generate Gherkin scenarios for a search feature that must: return results within 3 seconds, support keyword and phrase search, allow filtering by date range and category, and highlight matching terms in results.

28. Write Gherkin acceptance criteria for a file upload feature where users can upload documents up to 25MB, in PDF, DOCX, or XLSX format only, with a virus scan before acceptance.

29. Create Gherkin scenarios for a payment processing feature including: successful payment, declined card, insufficient funds, session timeout during payment, and duplicate transaction detection.

30. Write Gherkin acceptance criteria for a role-based access control system where: Admin users can create and deactivate accounts, Manager users can view team reports, Standard users can only access their own data.

31. Generate Gherkin scenarios for an auto-save feature that: saves form data every 60 seconds, preserves data on browser close, and prompts the user to restore their session on return.

32. Write Gherkin acceptance criteria for a multi-step form wizard that: validates each step before progression, displays a progress indicator, allows backward navigation without losing data, and sends a confirmation email on completion.

33. Create Gherkin scenarios covering the happy path and 3 error paths for a user login feature with MFA enabled.

34. Write Gherkin acceptance criteria for a report generation feature that must: handle datasets up to 500,000 rows, complete within 30 seconds, and provide a download link via email for large reports.

35. Generate Gherkin scenarios for a data import feature that: validates file structure, checks for duplicate records, displays a preview before import, logs all changes, and sends a summary report after completion.

### Acceptance Criteria — Conditions of Satisfaction

36. Write conditions of satisfaction for this user story: As a manager, I want to view a dashboard of my team's open tasks so that I can identify bottlenecks and reassign work as needed.

37. Generate non-functional acceptance criteria for a web application that must: load pages within 2 seconds, support 500 concurrent users, maintain 99.5% uptime, and pass an OWASP security scan.

38. Write acceptance criteria for a data synchronization feature that must: sync between the mobile app and server every 5 minutes when connected, queue changes when offline, and resolve conflicts using a last-write-wins rule.

39. Create acceptance criteria for an email notification system that: delivers within 5 minutes of trigger, includes an unsubscribe link, renders correctly in Outlook, Gmail, and Apple Mail, and logs delivery status.

40. Write conditions of satisfaction for an audit log feature covering: what events must be logged, required data fields, retention period, access controls, and export capability.

### Epic Decomposition & Story Splitting

41. This feature is too large for one sprint: "Build a complete vendor management module." Split it into 4–6 epics and break each epic into 3–4 user stories completable in a 2-week sprint.

42. I have a user story estimated at 13 story points: "As a user, I want to complete my profile so the system can personalize my experience." Split this into stories of 3 points or less, maintaining user value.

43. Decompose this workflow into user stories: A supervisor receives a daily digest of pending approvals, clicks through to review each item, can approve or reject with a comment, and the system notifies the requester of the decision.

44. Break down "Build an API integration with [third-party system]" into user stories covering: technical discovery, authentication setup, data mapping, error handling, testing, monitoring, and documentation.

45. I have an epic for a reporting module. Write the user stories for the MVP phase only — the smallest set of stories that still delivers real value to users.

### Story Refinement & QA

46. Review these user stories for ambiguity, missing actors, unclear benefits, and untestable criteria. Rewrite any that need improvement: [paste user stories].

47. These user stories were written by a business stakeholder. Rewrite them using proper BA structure: remove implementation details, add the "so that" benefit, and make them testable: [paste stories].

48. Identify the dependencies between these user stories and flag any that cannot be built independently: [paste list of user stories]. Suggest a recommended build sequence.

49. Write the Definition of Ready checklist for user stories in our Agile team. Include criteria for: scope clarity, acceptance criteria, design availability, dependency identification, and estimate consensus.

50. These user stories were each sized at 8 story points. Identify which can be split further and provide the split stories: [paste user stories].

### Edge Cases & Error Scenarios

51. I have this happy-path user story: [paste story]. Write 5 additional stories covering the key error scenarios, edge cases, and alternate flows.

52. Write user stories for the error handling behavior of a form submission when: the network drops mid-submit, a required field is blank, a file exceeds size limits, the session has expired, and the server returns an error.

53. Generate user stories covering accessibility edge cases for a form: a user navigating entirely by keyboard, a screen reader user, a user zoomed to 200%, and a user with a motor impairment using switch access.

54. Write user stories for the system behavior when a concurrent editing conflict occurs — two users editing the same record at the same time.

55. Create user stories for the data validation rules on a person record: name fields, date of birth, SSN format, phone number formats, email format, and address validation.

56. Write user stories for the system's behavior during scheduled maintenance windows: advance notification, graceful degradation, read-only mode, and restoration confirmation.

57. Generate user stories for offline functionality in a mobile application: what the user can do offline, what is restricted, how data syncs when connectivity returns, and how conflicts are handled.

58. Write user stories for timeout and session management: idle session warning, session expiration, data preservation on timeout, and re-authentication without data loss.

59. Create user stories for a cascading delete scenario: when a parent record is deleted, what happens to all related child records, dependent records, and historical audit data.

60. Write user stories for system behavior when an integration with [external system] is unavailable: fallback behavior, user messaging, retry logic, and data reconciliation after restoration.

### Specialized Story Types

61. Write infrastructure/DevOps user stories for a CI/CD pipeline covering: automated testing, deployment to staging, approval gates, production deployment, and rollback capability.

62. Generate data migration user stories for moving [X] years of historical records from a legacy system to a new platform, covering: data cleansing, mapping, validation, parallel run, and cutover.

63. Write security user stories for a web application covering: input validation, SQL injection prevention, XSS protection, CSRF protection, and penetration testing sign-off.

64. Create training and help content user stories: contextual help tooltips, searchable knowledge base, onboarding walkthrough, video tutorials, and printable user guides.

65. Write performance user stories defining system behavior under load: normal load, peak load, stress testing, graceful performance degradation, and recovery time objectives.

66. Generate integration user stories for connecting [System A] with [System B] via REST API: authentication, data mapping, error handling, retry logic, logging, and monitoring.

67. Write user stories for a data archiving process that: moves records older than [X] years to cold storage, maintains searchability, enforces access controls on archived data, and supports legal hold exceptions.

68. Create user stories for a multi-language and localization feature covering: language selection, date/number formatting, right-to-left text support, and translated content management.

69. Write user stories for a self-service reporting feature where non-technical users can: build reports using a visual interface, save templates, schedule automated delivery, and share reports with colleagues.

70. Generate user stories for an API documentation portal where developers can: browse endpoints, view request/response examples, test calls in a sandbox, and register for API keys.

