## CATEGORY 2: REQUIREMENTS GATHERING & ELICITATION
*80 Prompts*

### Elicitation Interview Questions

1. Generate 10 open-ended discovery questions for an initial stakeholder interview with an agency director about a new case management system. Focus on: current pain points, future vision, success metrics, and constraints.

2. I'm meeting with end users of a legacy system being replaced. Write 12 interview questions to understand their: current workflows, workarounds, what they value in the current system, and what they wish it did differently.

3. Write 8 probing follow-up questions for when a stakeholder says "the system should be user-friendly." These should help me define specific, measurable usability requirements.

4. Create 10 questions for a technical discovery session with the IT team covering: existing architecture, integrations, data structures, and technical constraints.

5. Generate interview questions for a session with a procurement officer about a new vendor management system. Include questions about: current process, pain points, regulatory requirements, transaction volume, and approval workflows.

6. Write 8 questions for a requirements session focused on security and compliance for a system that will process [PII/PHI/federal data]. Include: classification level, access controls, audit requirements, and applicable regulations.

7. I'm conducting requirements discovery for a data warehouse project. Generate 10 questions to ask business stakeholders about: reporting needs, data sources, analysis frequency, current tools, and decision-making use cases.

8. Create discovery questions for a meeting with the finance department about a new budget tracking system. Cover: budget cycle, approval hierarchies, reporting requirements, integrations with existing tools, and year-end processes.

9. Write elicitation questions for a session with field workers whose paper inspection form is being digitized. Focus on: their environment, connectivity, device preferences, data entry challenges, and form completion workflow.

10. Generate 10 questions for a session about a customer-facing portal. Focus on: customer demographics, digital literacy, accessibility needs, preferred channels, common issues with the current process, and what success looks like.

11. Write discovery questions to understand the reporting and analytics needs of an executive team. Focus on: what decisions they make, what data they currently use, how often they need updates, and what format they prefer.

12. Create interview questions to understand a complex approval workflow. Cover: number of steps, who approves at each step, criteria used, how exceptions are handled, and current bottlenecks.

13. Generate questions for a session about data migration requirements. Cover: what data exists, its quality, how it's structured, what needs to migrate vs. be archived, what validation is needed, and the acceptable cutover window.

14. Write questions to elicit non-functional requirements from a technical stakeholder. Cover: performance, scalability, availability, disaster recovery, security, maintainability, and compliance requirements.

15. Create questions for a kickoff meeting with a new client agency about a BA engagement. Cover: project goals, success criteria, key stakeholders, timeline constraints, budget boundaries, risks, and decision-making authority.

### Requirements Workshops

16. Design a 2-hour requirements workshop agenda for defining the scope of a new [system type] for a [agency type]. Include: icebreaker, current-state walkthrough, future-state vision, requirements prioritization, and next steps.

17. Write a set of structured questions to guide a joint requirements session with both business and technical stakeholders who have conflicting priorities about a new system.

18. Create a facilitation guide for a requirements prioritization workshop using MoSCoW method. Include: facilitator instructions, materials needed, ground rules, and a results capture template.

19. I facilitated a 3-hour requirements workshop and have these raw notes: [paste notes]. Synthesize the requirements, group related items, and format as a structured requirements list with categories.

20. Write the instructions and template for a "Day in the Life" workshop where users walk through their typical workday to help identify requirements and pain points.

21. Design a requirements validation walkthrough agenda where stakeholders review and confirm a requirements document. Include: how to collect feedback, manage disagreements, and reach consensus.

22. Create affinity mapping prompts and category headings for a workshop where 15 stakeholders contribute requirements for a new [system]. Use categories: Must-Haves, Nice-to-Haves, Concerns, and Questions.

23. Write a structured questionnaire for a requirements survey sent to 50 end users who can't attend a workshop. Cover: current process satisfaction, task frequency, pain points, and feature priorities.

24. I'm facilitating a workshop with stakeholders who have very different opinions about scope. Write ground rules, conflict resolution guidelines, and a decision-making framework for reaching consensus.

25. Design a "Future State Visioning" exercise where stakeholders describe what success looks like in 2 years without thinking about current limitations. Include facilitator prompts and an output capture template.

### Document Analysis

26. I have a copy of the current system's user manual: [paste excerpt]. Extract a list of functional capabilities that represent existing features we need to replicate or replace in the new system.

27. Analyze this existing report template: [paste template]. Identify all data fields, calculations, filters, and formatting requirements needed to replicate this report in a new system.

28. Review this current-state process document: [paste document]. Identify requirements for the new system by extracting: decision points, data inputs/outputs, roles, system interactions, and pain points.

29. I have the contract/SOW for a software implementation project: [paste relevant sections]. Extract the functional and non-functional requirements implied by the contract deliverables.

30. Analyze this regulatory document: [paste section]. Identify all requirements it imposes on a [system type] that processes [data type] for [agency type]. Format as a compliance requirements table with regulation references.

31. Review this data dictionary from the legacy system: [paste]. Identify which fields are critical to migrate, which are obsolete, and which need transformation for the new system.

32. Analyze this collection of user complaints and support tickets: [paste]. Group them by theme and identify the underlying requirements or system deficiencies they reveal.

33. I have meeting notes from the last 6 months of steering committee meetings: [paste]. Extract all decisions made, requirements implied, and constraints identified from these documents.

34. Review this draft RFP: [paste]. Identify requirements that are unclear, ambiguous, missing, or potentially contradictory. Suggest improved wording for each.

35. Analyze these screenshots from the current system: [describe screens]. Based on what you can see, write a list of functional requirements for a new system to replicate this functionality.

### Stakeholder Analysis

36. I'm starting a requirements project for a [system type] with these stakeholders: [list names and titles]. Create a stakeholder analysis matrix showing: influence level, interest level, requirements focus area, and recommended engagement approach.

37. Write a stakeholder communication plan for a 6-month requirements and design phase for a state agency IT modernization. Cover: frequency, format, audience, channel, and responsible party for each communication type.

38. I have conflicting requirements from two stakeholders: [describe conflict]. Write a structured summary of both positions, identify the underlying interests, and suggest 3 possible resolution approaches.

39. Generate questions to identify hidden stakeholders for a project affecting [department/agency]. Include questions about: downstream impacts, system integrations, regulatory oversight, and indirect users.

40. Write a RACI matrix for the requirements phase of a system development project with these roles: Business Sponsor, Program Manager, Business Analyst, Technical Lead, End User Representatives, Legal/Compliance, and IT Security.

41. A key stakeholder is disengaged and not responding to meeting invitations or review requests. Write an escalation email to their supervisor explaining the situation and the impact on the project timeline.

42. I need to present requirements to an executive who wants a 10-minute briefing. Write a one-page executive summary of these requirements: [paste requirements].

43. Write a requirements sign-off email explaining: what stakeholders are approving, the implications of approval, the change request process after sign-off, and the response deadline.

44. Two department heads disagree on the priority of a requirement. Write a decision memo presenting both perspectives, the trade-offs, and a recommendation for the project sponsor.

45. Create a requirements FAQ document answering the 10 most common questions stakeholders ask during a requirements review cycle.

### Business Rules Extraction

46. Review this policy document: [paste]. Extract all business rules implied by the policy and format them as structured statements using the format: IF [condition] THEN [action/result].

47. I need to document the decision logic for a loan eligibility check. Based on this description: [paste], write the business rules as a decision table showing conditions and outcomes.

48. Convert these narrative business rules into structured rule statements: [paste narrative]. Each rule should be unambiguous, testable, and independent of implementation details.

49. Write business rules for a document routing workflow where documents route to different approvers based on: document type, dollar amount, department, and urgency level.

50. Validate that these business rules are complete. Identify any gaps, contradictions, or missing conditions in this rule set: [paste rules].

### Requirements Organization & Documentation

51. Organize these raw requirements into a structured Business Requirements Document (BRD) with sections: Executive Summary, Business Objectives, Stakeholder Analysis, Scope, Functional Requirements, Non-Functional Requirements, Constraints, and Assumptions.

52. Write the Scope section of a BRD for a project to modernize the [agency]'s case management system. Include: what is in scope, what is explicitly out of scope, and the rationale for the boundaries.

53. These requirements came from multiple stakeholders and contain duplicates, conflicts, and gaps. Deduplicate them, flag conflicts, identify gaps, and organize by functional area: [paste requirements].

54. Write the Assumptions and Constraints section for a system modernization BRD. Include: technology constraints, regulatory constraints, resource constraints, timeline constraints, and key assumptions.

55. Convert this list of high-level requirements into a Requirements Traceability Matrix (RTM) template with columns: Requirement ID, Description, Source, Priority, Status, Test Case ID, and Design Reference.

56. Write a glossary of terms for a BRD for a [type] system in the [industry] sector. Include definitions for key business terms, acronyms, and technical terms used throughout the document.

57. I need to prioritize 30 requirements with my stakeholders. Write a requirements prioritization memo proposing a scoring approach based on: business value, implementation risk, regulatory requirement, and user impact.

58. Write the Business Objectives section of a BRD for a project to [objective]. Include 5–7 SMART objectives aligned to the organization's strategic goals.

59. Create a requirements change log template and write the change control process description for a requirements document that has been baselined.

60. Format these requirements into IEEE 830 Software Requirements Specification (SRS) structure: [paste requirements]. Use proper numbering, categories, and include a traceability table.

### Gap & Coverage Analysis

61. Review this requirements document: [paste]. Identify any functional areas that appear missing or underspecified compared to a typical [system type] implementation.

62. I have requirements for a new system and a list of features in the current system. Identify which current features are not covered by the new requirements: Current features: [list]. New requirements: [list].

63. Compare these requirements to the [regulation/standard] compliance checklist: [paste both]. Identify which compliance requirements are addressed, which are missing, and which are partially addressed.

64. Review the functional requirements for this system: [paste]. Identify any non-functional requirements that are implied but not explicitly stated (security, performance, accessibility, auditability).

65. I've drafted requirements for a user interface but may have missed some states. For a [form/screen type], identify all UI states that need requirements: empty, loading, error, success, disabled, read-only, and any others.

### Validation & Review

66. Review these requirements for testability. Identify any that contain vague terms like "user-friendly," "fast," or "should be easy" and rewrite them with specific, measurable criteria: [paste requirements].

67. Conduct a requirements peer review of this section: [paste]. Check for: completeness, consistency, correctness, feasibility, and verifiability. Format your findings as a review comment log.

68. These requirements were written 6 months ago and the business context has changed. Review them against this updated context: [paste context]. Identify which requirements are still valid, need updating, or can be removed.

69. Write a requirements validation checklist that the project team can use before sending requirements to stakeholders for sign-off.

70. I need to reconcile requirements from three different sources: stakeholder interviews, existing documentation, and regulatory requirements. Reconcile them into a single unified requirements list with sources noted: [paste all three].

### Elicitation Techniques

71. Design a card sorting exercise to help stakeholders prioritize 25 potential system features. Write: instructions, materials needed, facilitation guide, and how to interpret the results.

72. Write a structured observation guide for shadowing a [role] at a [agency type] for one day. Include: what to observe, what to record, and what questions to ask.

73. Create a prototype feedback questionnaire for collecting structured requirements input from users who have reviewed a low-fidelity mockup of a new [screen/feature].

74. Write instructions and an analysis template for a "5 Whys" root cause exercise to understand why users are currently doing manual workarounds instead of using the existing system.

75. Design a focus group discussion guide for gathering requirements from 8 end users about a new [feature] in a case management system. Include: opening questions, main topic questions, probing questions, and a wrap-up.

76. Write a requirements brainstorming session plan using the "How Might We" technique for identifying solutions to [identified problem]. Include facilitator instructions, prompts, and grouping methodology.

77. Create a structured questionnaire for evaluating whether an existing COTS product meets a set of requirements. Include: evaluation criteria, scoring methodology, and a comparison matrix template.

78. Write a "Day in the Life" observation report template for documenting requirements gathered from shadowing a [role type] in their work environment.

79. Design a requirements prioritization exercise using dot voting for 12 stakeholders choosing the top 10 priorities from 35 requirements. Include facilitation instructions and a scoring template.

80. Write a structured elicitation plan for a 3-month requirements phase using a combination of interviews, workshops, document analysis, and prototyping. Include: timeline, responsible parties, and deliverables for each activity.

